## Layout

Roast is one TypeScript package (ADR-0004): the CLI in `src/`, its
tests in `test/`, Node-run tooling in `scripts/`. Do not add Go tools.
The CI gate, in order:
`npm run lint`, `npm run typecheck`, `npm test` (builds `dist/cli.js`,
then runs the test bundle; it does not typecheck),
`git diff --exit-code -- roast.schema.json`, `npm pack --dry-run`.

## Area notes

Before editing code in an area, read its notes.

- Any code in `src/`, `test/`, or `scripts/`: `docs/agents/typescript.md`
- Config loading, roles, providers, the `init` starter,
  `roast.schema.json` (`src/config/`): `docs/agents/config.md`
- Trust and approvals (`src/trust/`, `*/trust.ts`):
  `docs/agents/trust.md`
- Stored credentials, `login`, `logout` (`src/credentials/`,
  `src/cli/*login*`, `src/cli/logout.ts`): `docs/agents/auth.md`
- Gate and the pre-commit hook (`src/gate/`): `docs/agents/gate.md`
- Agent skill install (`src/skill/`): `docs/agents/skill.md`
- Run records, `runs`, `ignore` (`src/record/`, `src/cli/runs.ts`):
  `docs/agents/records.md`
- Scope, context, exploration tools, snapshot denylist (`src/scope/`,
  `src/context/`, `src/snapshot/`): `docs/agents/scope-context.md`
- LLM adapters and the agent loop (`src/llm/`): `docs/agents/llm.md`

A new note goes in its area's file; a new area gets a file and a line
here. This file holds only what most tasks hit.

## Traps every task hits

- `npm run test:one` writes a shared bundle; run single-file suites
  sequentially to avoid executing the wrong suite.

- `JSON.parse` syntax errors can quote the input. Credential-file parse
  errors must use value-free messages so diagnostic output cannot expose
  a stored key.
- Shell hooks block Bash commands that contain a heredoc mentioning
  `process.env.PATH`, a heredoc or command containing `env:` or `.env`
  text, or a `credentials*` path together with `cat`, `sed`, `head`, and
  the like. Read and write such files with the editor tools.
- Review record initialization follows scope and prompt validation; early
  failures can leave no record, while later failures can retain evidence.
- Tests that write project state (run records, trust, stored
  credentials) chdir into a temporary Git repository first. `.roast/`
  ignores itself, so a leak into this checkout is invisible to
  `git status`; check with `ls -a`.
- CLI diagnostic tests that resolve roles need valid test credentials
  and local fake-server routing, even when asserting only local checks.
  Doctor fakes must route GET models checks and POST inference separately;
  a successful chat probe needs a lookup call followed by a final call
  that echoes the token from the replayed tool result.
- The CLI parser preserves dashed option names and disables boolean
  negation. Declare flags such as `no-browser` explicitly and read
  `args['no-browser']`, rather than a camel-case property.
- `init` login-offer tests that use `simulateTerminals(t, true)` must
  pass `--finder-model` and `--verifier-model` flags, otherwise
  interactive prompts consume stdin before the login offers see it.
- `simulateTerminals` only bypasses interactive-terminal detection. To
  exercise raw-mode prompt input through the CLI, give stdin an `isTTY`
  value of `true` and a `setRawMode` function; assert raw mode is restored.
- Browser-login tests send callbacks with the real fetch; the injected
  provider fetch rewrites every origin. Bound callback tests with
  `loginTimeouts.browser` and set injected display variables on Linux.
- After editing the bundled Roast skill, run `npm run test:one --
  test/skill/assets.ts` to check that bundled bytes match the source.
- Starter comments and runtime config/gate settings share defaults in
  `src/config/defaults.ts`. Review flag defaults should use that module
  too, so starter guidance stays aligned with runtime behavior.
- Login orchestration shares `ctx.lines` across providers and prompts.
  Reuse `providersNeedingLogin` and `loginProvider` for login offers;
  `loginPath` owns the scope rules shared by login and logout.

## Docs

- User-facing docs (`README.md`, `docs/`, the bundled
  `src/skill/assets/roast/SKILL.md`) write every invocation as
  `npx roast ...`; Roast is a project dependency, never a `PATH` binary.
  Settings are documented as `.roast.jsonc` keys, never environment
  variables; only credentials, `ROAST_SKIP`, and test-only `ROAST_LIVE_*`
  / `ROAST_TEST_*` variables exist.
- CLI messages in `src/cli/` (for example the gate hint printed
  during review) still say bare `roast`; changing them is a code change,
  not a docs change.
- Run records can retain arbitrary sensitive content. Metadata excludes
  resolved authentication material and response capture omits HTTP headers,
  but neither is comprehensive redaction of artifact content.
# Review model and finder

- `Finding` uses wire field names; empty `lines` becomes `null` through
  `toFinding`. Keep the ten wire fields in their schema declaration order.
- `Result` uses internal `dropped` and `stopReason` names. Report rendering
  owns the output names `dropped_findings` and `stop_reason`.
- Final decoders receive both a parsed value and optional raw argument
  text from `FinalSpec.decode`. Pass the raw text to the finding decoder
  to distinguish integer literals from decimal or exponent spellings.
  Early reports first round-trip the agent's float64 map through
  `encodeToolResult`; their integer spelling rules therefore differ.
- Fingerprints are similarity keys, never ids. Accumulation replaces
  content on a repeated fingerprint while preserving first-seen order.
  Capture runs on every accepted add, including submission merges.
- Finder unit tests live in `test/review/`; scripted client tests reuse
  `test/llm/agent-helpers.ts`, and HTTP tests use the inference server.
  Import new test modules from `test/index.ts`.
# Roast

An adversarial, cross-model code reviewer installed as a project dependency
via npm. One model proposes defects in a Git diff and a second model checks
surviving candidates when verification runs.

## Language

### Review

**Finder**:
The role whose model reads the diff and proposes candidate findings.
_Avoid_: reviewer, author model

**Verifier**:
The role whose model checks candidate findings against the cited code. It
can confirm, refute, or report insufficient context for a finding.
_Avoid_: checker, judge

**Role**:
One of the two review positions, finder or verifier, each bound to one
inference profile.

**Inference profile**:
A named, complete unit of model, endpoint, protocol, output limit and
credential source.
_Avoid_: provider, backend

**Run record**:
The stored evidence of one review, kept inside the project that was reviewed.
It can contain sensitive reviewed content, prompts, arguments, and provider
responses; excluding resolved authentication material from dedicated metadata
does not make the recorded content credential-free.
_Avoid_: log, history

**Gate**:
The pre-commit hook that runs a quick review of staged content and fails open.
_Avoid_: pre-commit check, hook

### Configuration

**Project config**:
The `.roast.jsonc` file (JSONC) nearest the working directory, searched upward
no further than the Git toplevel. It is the single home for every non-secret
setting.
_Avoid_: repo config, local config, rc file

**Trusted project config**:
A project config whose security-relevant contents (non-default endpoints,
credential variable names, prompt-override files) the user has explicitly
approved for this clone. Roast does not send code or credentials anywhere a
project config names until it is trusted.
_Avoid_: allowed config, whitelisted config

**Credential**:
An API key. Credentials are the only settings that come from outside the
project config: from environment variables, or from a stored credential. A
project config may name the variable but never hold the value.
_Avoid_: secret, token

**Built-in provider**:
A provider Roast knows by name (OpenAI, Anthropic, OpenRouter): its
endpoint, the protocols it accepts there, its standard key variable, and
how to log in.
A selected profile on a built-in provider's endpoint with an accepted
protocol and its standard or implicit key variable requires no approval on
its own; other selected profiles and prompt overrides also matter.
_Avoid_: default provider, known provider

**Stored credential**:
A built-in provider's key kept in a credentials file, scoped to the project
(inside the Git directory) or to the user. It ranks below every environment
variable and is sent only to that provider's own endpoint (ADR-0002).
_Avoid_: saved key, cached token
# TypeScript

Applies to `src/`, `test/`, and `scripts/`. Notes for one area are in
that area's file (index in `AGENTS.md`).

## Tooling

- `npm test` builds `dist/cli.js`, then bundles and runs the tapzero
  suite; it does not typecheck. A new test module must be imported from
  `test/index.ts`. esbuild drops unused imports without resolving them,
  so only `npm run typecheck` catches a broken import.
- `npm run test:one -- test/<area>/<file>.ts` bundles one test module
  to `test/one-bundle.js` and runs it through tap-spec. Test modules
  cannot run directly under Node: their `.js` import specifiers only
  resolve once bundled. It does not typecheck; run `typecheck` too.
- Lint with `npm run lint`: it preloads `scripts/eslint-ts6.ts`, which
  gives eslint the TypeScript 6 API because typescript-eslint throws on
  the TypeScript 7 that `typecheck` uses.
- Node runs `scripts/` directly (type stripping), so they use only
  erasable syntax: no enums, namespaces, or parameter properties.
- `baseOptions` in `scripts/esbuild-options.ts` holds the esbuild
  options every bundle shares (text loaders for `.tmpl`, `.md`, `.sh`;
  `mainFields: ['module', 'main']`, which `jsonc-parser`'s UMD build
  needs); `scripts/bundle.ts` adds the `require` shim banner. A test
  that bundles code itself uses both.
- `npm run build` also writes `dist/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt`
  (`scripts/licenses.ts`) and fails, naming the package, when a bundled
  package lacks an allowlisted `license` field or a license file. A new
  runtime library must pass it; extending `ALLOWED_LICENSES` needs
  maintainer approval. `@substrate-system/debug` fails it today, so no
  `src/` module may import it yet. Runtime libraries are
  `devDependencies` (bundled); `dependencies` stays empty.

## Source conventions

- Keep source ASCII: write `\uXXXX` escapes. Some editing tools decode
  escapes into literal characters when writing; check with
  `grep -nP '[^\x00-\x7f]'` after writing a file. eslint `--fix`
  (quote-props) rewrites a quoted `'\u00e9'` object key into a literal
  non-ASCII identifier; put such keys in a const and use a computed key.
- eslint rejects control characters in regex literals
  (`no-control-regex`) and labeled statements (`no-labels`); scan char
  codes and use helper functions instead.
- Startup guards live in `src/startup.ts`, not `src/cli.ts`: importing
  the entry runs the CLI, so testable helpers sit in other modules.
- Module-level swappable functions use a mutable hooks object plus a
  delegating export (`terminalHooks`, `skillHooks.lookPath`), because
  tests cannot reassign an ESM `let` export. Swap the object's field
  and restore it in `t.cleanup`.
- Shared helpers; import these, never re-implement:
  `src/gocompat/` (`strings.ts`: Go TrimSpace/Ext/`%.2f`/strconv.Quote/
  byte-order sort and `truncateUtf8`; `duration.ts`: Go durations as
  bigint nanoseconds; `match.ts`: Go `path.Match`),
  `src/textsafe/textsafe.ts`, `src/fsutil/atomic.ts`
  (`writeFileAtomic`, options cover trust, credentials, and gate temp
  names and error messages), `src/git/git.ts` (the only git runner;
  pass the CLI context's `env`, it reads `process.env` only when none is
  given), `src/util/pool.ts` (`mapPool`).

## CLI core (`src/cli/`)

- Commands are `CommandDef`s (`command.ts`) listed in `commands.ts`;
  `run.ts` builds the yargs tree from that list plus `registerCommand`
  (tests), and calls the handler only after parsing succeeds.
- A handler gets a `RunContext` (cwd, env, signal, fetch, one shared
  `LineReader`) and throws `UsageError` / `ExitSignal` / any error; it
  never writes the error itself or reads process globals.
- A group with `subcommands` and no handler prints its help.
- yargs number parsing is off (`parse-numbers: false`). Declare an
  integer flag as `type:'string'` and parse decimal digits in the
  handler, throwing `UsageError` on a bad value (see `parseLimit` in
  `runs.ts`).
- Terminal detection and browser opening are swapped through
  `terminalHooks` (`terminal.ts`).
- With only the default `$0` command registered, yargs omits the
  Commands section from root help; it appears once a second command
  registers.

## Review command (`src/cli/review.ts`)

- End-to-end review tests use `settingsRepo` (`test/helpers/review.ts`):
  a temp repo with an uncommitted change, a config on a fake `chat`
  server, keys in env, `review.snapshot: false`, and approval via
  `roast trust --yes`. It chdirs into the repo.
- yargs keeps only the last occurrence of an array flag under
  `duplicate-arguments-array: false`; review slice flags are re-read
  from `ctx.argv` (`review-flags.ts`). Flags whose "changed" state
  matters have no yargs default (`undefined` = not given).
- The structured finder call offers `findings` plus the agent's
  `__roast_continue`; assert tool presence, not exact lists.
- Verification (step 17) runs only with `doVerify` and at least one
  finding. `verify` (`src/verify/verify.ts`) never throws; an abort
  becomes undecided verdicts, so the run still writes its record and
  exits 4. Its result types `notes` as `string[]|null` (Go parity), so
  CLI code after step 17 takes `VerifiedResult`, not `Result`.
- Verifier CLI tests (`test/cli/review-verify.ts`) put the verifier on
  its own profile and model and tell its requests apart by the
  `submit_verdict` tool. The default role key variable
  (`ROAST_VERIFIER_API_KEY`) backs any profile, so an invalid-key test
  must delete it from the env.
- The entry exits within the tick of the first SIGINT, and identical
  signals sent back to back coalesce, so a second-signal test needs a
  slow wind-down (a fake `git` that exits slowly on SIGTERM).

## Tests

- Use the `test` wrapper in `test/helpers/test.ts`, which adds Go-style
  `t.cleanup` (tapzero has no teardown hook). tapzero's `Test` has no
  `notOk`; write `t.ok(!x, ...)`.
- CLI tests run in-process with `runRoast` (`test/helpers/run.ts`),
  which makes a temp repo with an isolated env (`test/helpers/repo.ts`:
  `tempRepo`, `tempDir`, which also sets `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES`).
  `test/cli/process.ts` and `test/tooling/bundle.ts` spawn `dist/cli.js`,
  so they need a fresh `npm run build` (`npm test` does that).
- Test environments come from `isolatedEnv`, which starts from
  `cleanEnv(process.env)` (`test/helpers/env.ts`): every `ROAST_*`,
  `OPENAI_*`, `OPENROUTER_*`, and `ANTHROPIC_*` variable is stripped. A
  new provider whose key variable has another prefix goes in its
  `AMBIENT_PREFIXES`. Tests set credentials in `io.env`, never in
  `process.env`.
- A review test needs an approved `.roast.jsonc` on the fake server:
  `settingsRepo` approves, or call `approveProjectConfig`. A test
  asserting a config error approves a valid config and then edits it,
  so a trust failure cannot mask the error. A doctor test that probes a
  custom endpoint approves in its temp repo (`approveDoctorConfig`,
  `test/cli/doctor.ts`), never in this checkout.
- Untracked files are part of a review's diff, so a test that checks
  which file's text reached the fake server keeps decoys out of scope
  (for example through `.git/info/exclude`).
- Fakes: `startInferenceServer` (`test/helpers/inference-server.ts`)
  speaks the three protocols; `providerServer`
  (`test/helpers/provider-server.ts`) adds key-check and OpenRouter
  routes. `runRoast({ redirectTo })` uses `redirectingFetch`, so a
  profile on a built-in endpoint reaches the fake. `requestRecorder` and
  `reviewMeta` (`test/helpers/review.ts`) read what the fake received
  and the run's `meta.json`.
- Swap terminals and the browser with `simulateTerminals` /
  `captureBrowser` (`test/helpers/terminal.ts`).
- Tests that spawn processes use `test/helpers/spawn.ts` (`ROOT`,
  `run`).
- Git runner tests observe a child by putting a fake `git` shell script
  first on the injected `PATH` (spawn resolves the command through
  `env.PATH`); see `test/git/git.ts`.
- Live tests live under `test/live/` (entry `index.ts`,
  `npm run test:live`); each passes with a "skipped" note when its
  `ROAST_LIVE_*` key is unset, so the TAP stream is never empty.
# LLM layer and agent loop (TS)

`src/llm/`.

## Adapters

- Adapters read no environment variable. A protocol is an entry in
  `ADAPTERS` (`client.ts`) with a pure encoder and an
  `AsyncIterable<SseEvent>` decoder returning a `ModelTurn`; `send` owns
  fetch, headers (`http.ts` `requestHeaders`), capture, usage, and
  abort.
- One `errors.ts`: `LlmError.kind` (underscore names, read by
  `src/cli/errors.ts`) and `sentinels` (hyphen names, `hasSentinel`)
  must stay in step.
- Tool results sent to models go through `encodeToolResult` (`json.ts`)
  only.
- `anthropic` (`anthropic.ts`) groups items into alternating role
  messages first, then drops a thinking-only assistant message; it never
  edits, reorders, or drops a signed block. `tool_choice` matches Go
  (`{type: auto, disable_parallel_tool_use: false}`).
- Usage stage 1 lives in `usage.ts` per protocol (`chatUsage`,
  `responsesUsage`, `anthropicUsage`); decoders call `normalizeUsage`
  on its result.
- Making an adapter available changes tests that pin unavailability:
  the construction test in `test/llm/client.ts` and the doctor roundtrip
  test (`test/cli/doctor.ts`, whose `doctorProbe` must speak the
  protocol).

## Agent loop (`agent.ts`, `runConversation`)

- `createClient(modelClient, {ledger, now, sleep, sessionId})`
  (`client.ts`) wraps a `ModelClient` into the finder/verifier `Client`.
  The pure decision is `reviewResponse` (`review.ts`); the deadline,
  ledger, final-argument validation, and retries are separate modules.
- `ModelClient.send` owns the request timeout; the loop composes only
  caller + deadline/window signals.
- Cutoff errors come from `cutoffError` (`errors.ts`), which keeps the
  cause out of the matchable chain; tests assert `details.stopCause`
  kinds, never message text.

## Tests

- `test/helpers/inference-server.ts` (`startInferenceServer`: a script
  picks a reply per request; it records method, path, headers, raw and
  parsed body) and `inference-stream.ts` (`writeInferenceStream`, the
  native SSE per protocol). The `responses` writer, like Go's, emits
  every output item as a function call, so message items yield no text.
- `test/helpers/anthropic-fakes.ts`: `anthropicCalls` (a Messages
  response), `startBindingServer` (rejects a signed thinking block whose
  prefix changed; counts `rejected`), and `deepSeekScript` /
  `startDeepSeekServer` (400 on a forced tool choice or a missing
  reasoning replay). `deepSeekScript` plugs into `settingsRepo`, whose
  fetch rewrites every origin to its own server.
- A fake that answers a different protocol than the request path makes
  the client see an incomplete stream and retry with the 5 s backoff;
  branch on `req.path` when one script serves two protocols.
- CLI review tests on `responses` use `responsesProfileConfig`,
  `responsesFindingsReply`, and `responsesCalls` (`test/helpers/review.ts`).
  Requests through `runRoast`'s rewriting fetch carry
  `transfer-encoding`; filter it with the other transport headers.
- Byte-level body behavior (read-ahead, caps, cancel counts) is tested
  with a `highWaterMark: 0` `ReadableStream` returned through
  `ClientConfig.fetch`; `new Response(stream)` neither copies nor
  pre-reads it.
- Agent-loop tests drive `harness(script)` from
  `test/llm/agent-helpers.ts` (a scripted fake `ModelClient`: canned
  turns, errors, or functions of the request; sleeps recorded).
- `AbortSignal.timeout` timers do not keep Node alive, so a fake step
  that waits for an abort must hold a timer (the `aborted()` helper
  does); otherwise the test process just exits.
